                "reviews": [
                    {
                        "recommendationid": "22698450",
                        "author": {
                            "steamid": "76561197988023670",
                            "num_reviews": 4,
                            "playtime_forever": 241,
                            "playtime_last_two_weeks": 0,
                            "playtime_at_review": 224,
                            "last_played": 1463628048
                        },
                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Since HoloBall just came out a few days before Cyberpong, I think many people will be wondering what the differences are and if it's worth owning both.\n\nIn single player, Cyberpong VR is best described as VR Breakout with a dash of Tetris.  Blocks spawn at the back of the arena and slowly move towards you.  You destroy them with bouncing balls and powerups until a block makes it to your side.  Then the game is over.  There's strategy in how you save and use the powerups, and skill in juggling and aiming the many balls.\n\nThe suprise for me was PvP multiplayer, where it plays like VR Pong.  There are no powerups or blocks, just returning the ball back and forth as it becomes faster and faster.  It sounds simple, and it is, but being face to face in VR with your opponent brings so much to the game.  It's an absolute blast.  Shoutout to Shaquille who beat me easily 5 games straight.\n\nCyberpong will have you moving all around the required play area, but it doesn't push you to swing with the same vigor that HoloBall does.  I've had to take a break for a few days from HoloBall for my arm to recover.  Cyberpong brings my pulse up, but doesn't wear me out.\n\nTL;DR: While Cyberpong VR and HoloBall look similar, they play very differently, and both are great fun.  The online multiplayer in Cyberpong VR is an absolute must try.",
